Core Concept
Assassin Order Simulator — an open-world pixel-style survival-craft-build management sim: gathering, building, recruiting, crafting weapons/poison, and taking commissions that escalate from small jobs to history-changing contracts.

Story Premise
The MC is a disabled crown prince/princess (blind, with a limp) banished after a coup. They flee with one loyal servant and build an assassin guild in secret to destroy the dynasty that took everything. The MC's divine gift is clairvoyant sight within limited range, and the power to forge a life-contract with masters — compelling absolute loyalty ("cannot betray, must protect with their lives") that lasts until the MC's death. Since the main story doesn't kill the MC off, every recruited master is effectively permanent. The MC operates as a hidden leader; only masters know their true identity, and the first master (the loyal servant) becomes their eyes, ears, and hands.

Core Mechanics
Dual-mode actions: assign an assassin (idle/timer, safe but slow) or go personally via master (active, risky — failure benches that master temporarily)
Masters: gacha-collectible, one on-duty at a time, passive skill + active mission kit
Assassins: plentiful roster, simpler stats, specialties (poison, ranged, infiltration, brute)
World: zone-based overworld for mobile feasibility, but open enough for freeform building anywhere on the map (Medieval Dynasty–style) — including outside the main castle

Anti-Monotony Strategy
Freedom of base identity/specialization (poison-guild vs. weapon-guild vs. self-sufficient) gives macro purpose
Story chapters arrive as patch-based branches in new zones — each branch has a genuinely different core mechanic/mini-game (Arknights-event style), not just reskinned systems, while sharing a stable "core grammar" (gather → produce → deliver) so players don't relearn the game each time
Branches require assigning a master away from the main castle; branch resources feed back into the main castle and unlock higher-tier commissions; the main castle expands as branches develop
Branch order is fixed/authored, not player-chosen, tied to story progression

Scattered Open-World Building
Players can build anywhere on the map (like hunting lodges in Medieval Dynasty), but placement is a real strategic decision — bad spots risk discovery and destruction by the ruling authority. This reinforces both the "spatial management" layer and the hidden-rebellion narrative theme. Still to define: what makes a spot good/risky, warning system before destruction, stakes when a base is lost, and rewards for long-term successful concealment.

Retention Philosophy
Explicitly not chasing daily-quest-driven login habits. Instead aiming for "I belong there" motivation — a persistent, living world worth checking on (ambient changes, visible ownership/growth, character moments) rather than reward-gated tasks. Scoped as an indie project: "good enough" for solid monetization and experience, not competing with AAA live-service retention systems.

Freedom-Driven Emergent Gameplay
Open-ended systems naturally produce funny/emergent player stories — a "master assassin guild" that's secretly a farm, attachment/jokes around specific masters, deliberately bad base placements as challenge runs, solo/minimalist playstyles. This works because narratively "serious" characters (bound-by-contract operatives) have total mechanical freedom to be redirected — the gap between intended and player-chosen role is the comedy.
 
Alchemy, Seed Exploration, Smithing
Alchemy (unknown-recipe herb crafting) and seed exploration (personal master forest exploration, plant-to-reveal) were discussed as deep optional systems tied to the farmer identity. Smithing (tool tiers gating efficiency, 2048-style weapon merge-crafting) was recommended as the one deep system to fully build for v1, with alchemy/seed-exploration deferred to post-launch.
 
Money & Black Market Economy
Money is a universal-but-imperfect equalizer — anything can be bought, but the market buys cheap/sells expensive, and rare purchases carry detection risk tied to the concealment/authority theme. Risk consequence: master imprisonment. Refined into a heat/suspicion meter (systemic, not per-purchase gambling) rather than flat dice rolls.
 
Master Economic Specialization (Gacha Hook)
Black-market price/risk bonuses gated behind master skills obtainable via gacha, giving SSR (or scaled R/SR/SSR) masters value as economic specialists — a new gacha-pull axis beyond combat, reinforcing "different identities, all viable."
 
V1 Scope Definition
Decided v1 should include all core systems except the branch/patch system, sequenced naturally through the story: survival start → hut → hideout → recruiting → minor commissions → growing guild → climax commission. Specifically excavation/mining, farming, and smithing (capped at mid-tier) are v1 systems; alchemy/poison is explicitly deferred to the branch/patch system, not launch.
 
V1 Story Arc
MC (disabled royal in hiding) starts alone with one master, survives, builds a hut → hideout, recruits assassins, takes minor commissions against commoner targets, escalating to a climax commission: assassinating a corrupt town/village head with a dangerous agenda to destroy the town. Ending: the head is replaced with an ally of the assassin order, making the main castle's surrounding area safe from authority detection going forward — a satisfying resolution that also sets up branches as where risk/tension re-escalates in new zones.

Sovereign — the MC (hidden royal, clairvoyant, stationary/hidden leader). Commands the order without ever appearing directly.
In-world epithet: "Eyes of the Mountain" — a whispered legend/rumor title used by others (NPCs, folklore, old texts), not a mechanical UI label. Echoes the historical "Old Man of the Mountain" (Sheikh al-Jabal) while naming the MC's actual clairvoyant power instead of just borrowing the original title wholesale.
 
Master (replaces "master") — bound by life-contract, gacha-collectible, personal/active-mission characters. One on-duty at a time.
Reads clean in UI ("Recruit a new Master," "Your Master was knocked out").
Historical root: maps to the Da'i (and senior Da'i al-Kabir) of the real Nizari Ismaili order — trusted, personally vetted field commanders operating with real autonomy one level below the hidden supreme leader.
 
Climax Item: The Obsidian Blade
The climax commission requires an obsidian blade, obtainable through two (now three) paths:
1. Craft it — via committed mining + smithing investment (mid-tier crafting payoff)
2. Buy it — attempting this on the black market fails: the seller refuses and reports the player, resulting in the master being detained (not just risked) — a scripted betrayal beat that punishes trying to "buy your way out," consistent with the story's theme of corrupt shortcuts. but player can buy the resources to craft the blade.
3. Broker it via diplomacy — training/using a high-skill (ideally SSR) diplomat master allows successfully negotiating the purchase instead, giving money/social-focused players a legitimate third path without being forced into crafting
 
This makes the climax a genuine convergence point: every playstyle needs some real investment (crafting mastery or diplomatic mastery) to succeed, rather than money alone trivializing the final challenge — preserving the "money can't solve everything" rule at the moment it matters most.
 
Recap: What's confirmed for v1
- Core survival/building loop (hut → hideout → castle, free placement)
- Excavation/mining (dual-mode: personal master vs. assigned assassin)
- Farming (basic resources; seed exploration optional/discovery-based, without alchemy ties yet)
- Smithing + 2048-style weapon merge-crafting (capped at mid-tier, with obsidian as a special climax-tier exception)
- Money & black market economy, including heat/suspicion risk and master imprisonment consequence
- Master gacha system, including at least one economic-specialist (negotiation/diplomacy) skill line
- Full story arc from solo survival through the corrupt town head climax
- Explicitly excluded from v1: alchemy/poison crafting, branch/patch zones — both reserved for post-launch content

## V1 Main Quest Draft
 
### **Chapter 1 — Exile**
**Story:** The MC (disabled crown prince/princess) survives a coup, fleeing into the wilderness with one loyal servant. Blind, with a limp, but carrying a hidden divine gift — clairvoyant sight within limited range.
**Objective:** Survive the first days — build a hut, gather basic resources.
**Systems unlocked:** Basic gathering (wood/food), basic building (hut), intro to dual-mode (servant does the physical gathering; MC's clairvoyance "sees" nearby resources/danger).
**Beat:** The servant is bound as the MC's first master via life-contract — the game teaches what this bond means narratively (loyalty, "eyes/ears/hands").
 
### **Chapter 2 — Foundation**
**Story:** Hut becomes the seed of a hideout. The MC resolves to rebuild in secret and begin their revenge.
**Objective:** Expand hut into a basic hideout; establish first resource loops.
**Systems unlocked:** Free-placement building expands, mining/excavation introduced, farming introduced (including optional seed exploration), basic smithing (tools gate efficiency — e.g., need an axe to chop faster).
**Beat:** First hints of the world beyond the forest — rumors of a corrupt town nearby, players get their first sense of the political landscape.
 
### **Chapter 3 — First Blood**
**Story:** The guild needs funding and purpose. The MC authorizes their first assassination contracts — small, personal, against commoners (though "commoner" targets subtly seed the larger conspiracy).
**Objective:** Recruit a second master (first gacha pull moment), accept and complete minor commissions.
**Systems unlocked:** Full assassin roster/recruitment, commission system (assign vs. personal dual-mode missions), money economy introduced (commission payouts, basic buying).
**Beat:** A completed commission reveals a clue connecting the target to the town head's agenda — first thread of the main conspiracy.
 
### **Chapter 4 — The Guild Grows**
**Story:** Reputation spreads quietly. More recruits arrive, resource needs scale up.
**Objective:** Grow roster, expand hideout into a proper base, unlock mid-tier smithing (merge-crafting weapons).
**Systems unlocked:** Full merge-crafting mechanic, black market access (buy/sell, heat/suspicion meter introduced), master economic specialization skills become relevant.
**Beat:** Clues accumulate — the town head isn't just corrupt, but actively planning something that endangers the whole town (smuggling disasters, sabotage, or a deliberate famine/fire — pick one as the concrete "dangerous agenda").
 
### **Chapter 5 — The Net Tightens**
**Story:** The conspiracy comes into full view. The MC learns the scope of the town head's plan and the timeline before it's too late.
**Objective:** Gather intelligence, complete escalating commissions against the town head's key allies/protectors (bodyguards, corrupt officials, informants).
**Systems unlocked:** Higher-tier commissions, full black market risk stakes (a failed/risky purchase could cost a master here as real tension-building before the climax).
**Beat:** A master may be lost to imprisonment here (or narrowly avoided) — a good place for a rescue-mission side-beat if you want it, raising emotional stakes before the finale.
 
### **Chapter 6 — The Obsidian Path**
**Story:** The final commission is authorized: assassinate the town head before their plan executes. But the weapon required — the obsidian blade — isn't something ordinary steel can replace.
**Objective:** Player must, through whichever playstyle they've built, obtain the obsidian blade:
 - **Craft it** (mining + smithing mastery), or
 - **Broker it** (diplomat master negotiates the purchase), or
 - **Attempt to buy it outright** and fail (seller refuses, reports the master — detained, forcing the player onto one of the other two paths)
**Systems converge here:** every major system built over the game (mining, smithing, economy, master specialization) funnels into this single required moment.
 
### **Chapter 7 — The Reckoning**
**Story:** With the obsidian blade obtained, the MC (via chosen master, personal active mission) completes the climax assassination.
**Objective:** Execute the final commission against the town head.
**Beat:** Resolution — the corrupt head is replaced by an ally of the assassin order. The area around the main castle becomes safe from authority detection. The MC's guild has its first real, permanent foothold, and the revenge quest against the wider dynasty properly begins.
**End of V1:** Epilogue beat hinting at the larger conspiracy/dynasty (Book 2 hook) and possibly a tease of the first branch/zone to come.

Chapter 1 — Exile (Detailed Draft)

Opening state: MC wakes/arrives in the wilderness, disoriented, injured or freshly escaped. Blind, with a limp — establish this physically before anything else (movement is slow, screen/vision is limited or obscured to communicate blindness without frustrating the player). The loyal servant is already present — they didn't get separated, they fled together.

Beat 1 — Establishing the bond (before any real gameplay)
A short narrative/dialogue scene: the servant pledges to stay, the MC reveals (or the servant already knows) their identity. This is where the life-contract is formed — not as a menu/system explanation yet, just an emotional beat: "I bind you not as master to servant, but eyes to the blind, hands to the still." Keep the mechanical explanation (loyalty, permanence) light here; let it land as devotion first, mechanic second.

Beat 2 — Teaching clairvoyance as the core "vision" mechanic
Immediately after, let the player physically feel the MC's blindness/limp limitation, then introduce clairvoyance as the workaround — a limited-range "reveal" around the MC showing nearby resources/dangers, while anything outside that range is genuinely hidden until the servant (master) physically scouts it. This single moment teaches your whole dual-mode philosophy wordlessly: MC senses, master acts.

Beat 3 — First gather/build loop (tutorial pacing)

Direct the servant to gather wood (assign-and-wait, your idle dual-mode) or let the player control the servant personally for the first gather (active dual-mode) — I'd suggest starting with personal/active control for the very first action, since new players learn by doing, not delegating; introduce the assign option only after they understand the base action.
Build the first hut — simple, minimal choices (maybe literally one placement decision) just to introduce the free-placement building system without overwhelming a brand-new player with the full scattered-open-world complexity yet.

Beat 4 — First night / survival tension
A small survival pressure beat (cold, hunger, or a distant danger glimpsed via clairvoyance) that justifies why the hut matters right now — gives the early loop stakes beyond "because the tutorial said so."

Beat 5 — Chapter close / transition hook
Hut is standing, first night survived. Close on a quiet, human moment between MC and servant (this is your best chance to make the player care about the relationship before the game opens up into systems) and a forward-look line — a rumor, a distant light, a sound — that seeds Chapter 2's "world beyond the forest" beat.

Design notes to keep in mind for this chapter specifically:

This is your one chapter where linearity is good. Every later chapter should open up freedom-based play; Chapter 1's job is purely to teach the core verbs (gather, build, dual-mode, clairvoyance) in a guided, low-choice way before Chapter 2 starts loosening the reins.
Keep systems minimal on purpose — no commissions, no money, no roster yet. Just: move, gather, build, and the MC/master relationship. Every extra system here dilutes the emotional focus of "just the two of us against the world," which is your strongest opening hook.
The blindness mechanic needs to feel meaningful, not gimmicky — worth deciding concretely how vision-limitation actually renders (fog outside clairvoyance range, muted/grayscale outside range with color inside, etc.) since this is a core identity feature of your MC, not just a stat.
